Standards are listed as those normally cited for this component class. They describe the requirement, not a claim of certification. Test evidence applicable to a specific order is confirmed per purchase order.

## 6. Handling and installation

- Cut the screen back to the drawing length. A screen that is short leaves an unbonded region; a screen that is long crowds the stress cone.
- Do not move the sleeve once the stress cone has passed the screen end. Repositioning breaks the interface that controls the field.
- Keep the cable insulation free of grease, pencil marks and moisture before recovery.
- Confirm the earthing connection to the screen or armour is made and torqued before the joint is closed.

## 7. Failure modes and mitigation

| Failure mode | Likely cause | Mitigation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Partial discharge at the screen cut | Screen cut-back outside tolerance or an uneven cut edge concentrating stress | Cut to the drawing length, deburr the screen edge, and verify with a PD check if the project requires one. |
| Surface tracking on an outdoor termination | Insufficient creepage for the pollution level, or contamination on the sheds | Match the shed profile to the site pollution class and clean the outer surface before energisation. |
| Moisture path into the joint | Sealing at the sheath or the lug end left incomplete | Apply mastic and sealing parts to drawing and confirm the sheath is clean and dry. |
| Loose recovered wall | Accessory selected against conductor size while the insulation OD is out of range | Select on the measured insulation diameter. |

## 8. Typical applications

- Utility and industrial MV distribution networks and ring main units.
- Renewable collection systems where terminations are made in the field.
- Data centre and infrastructure MV connections with short outage windows.
- Switchgear and transformer interfaces where an indoor and an outdoor variant are both needed.

